window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][13] = "<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A bike-share company in a mid-sized city finds that 38% of new riders never take a second ride within 30 days (invented). The operations team believes the cause is bike availability and wants to buy more bikes. The product team is asked to design a better first-ride experience for new riders before the company commits budget.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A fixed budget for the quarter, an app team of four, and docks and bikes that cannot be redesigned this year. Any change must work on the existing docks and on a phone with a weak signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> App logs show that 31% of first-time release attempts fail on the first try (invented). The team watches 12 first rides at three docks and finds that nine riders spend more than two minutes at the dock working out how to release the bike or adjust the seat, and five give up and walk away (invented). A short survey of 400 lapsed riders shows 22% citing &quot;no bikes available&quot; and 41% citing &quot;confusing or stressful the first time&quot; (invented). Several watch the in-app clock run while they struggle.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> The belief was that new riders leave because bikes are not available. The evidence points to the first two minutes at the dock: new riders face an unfamiliar release step with the price clock running, and some give up before riding. Written as an observable pain point: &quot;A first-time rider at a dock cannot tell how to release the bike, sees the fare clock running, and abandons the ride.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> Availability matters to 22% of lapsed riders, and the observed behavior at the dock is stronger evidence than the survey, because it shows what riders do and not what they remember. The riders who struggled described a worry that &quot;the clock is already running,&quot; which makes a small confusion expensive. They have workarounds (asking a passer-by, calling support) that are signs of real pain. The pain differs from the operations team's belief in a way that shifts the solution from buying hardware to guiding a first-time moment. The riskiest assumption is that new riders would complete the release if they were guided and not charged while learning.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Buy more bikes</td>\n<td>Availability, the operations team's belief</td>\n<td>A large share of the quarter's budget; does not touch the first-ride problem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. A first-ride mode in the app: a two-step release guide with a short animation, and the fare clock starts when the bike is released, not when the rider scans</td>\n<td>The first two minutes</td>\n<td>About three weeks of app work; a small revenue change from the free minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. A sticker with the steps on each dock</td>\n<td>Visible help for people who do not open the app</td>\n<td>Printing and fitting; no guidance for the clock worry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. B and C together</td>\n<td>Coverage</td>\n<td>More cost; blurs what worked</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B directly addresses the observed cause and costs a few weeks. It gives up the possibility that availability matters in some zones, which can be tested later. C is cheap and works without the app, so it helps people who do not have it open, but it cannot fix the clock worry. A is the most expensive and the least connected to the evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Build B for new riders only, and add C at three docks as a cheap comparison. Primary metric: the share of new riders who complete a second ride within 30 days. Input: the first-attempt release success rate. <a href=\"#gl-guardrail-metric\">guardrail metric</a>: the safety incident rate for first rides (a guided rider might rush the seat adjustment on a curb), and the revenue per ride for new riders. Counter metric: the rate of new riders who ride once, with the free minutes, and never again.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario the first-attempt release success rate rose from 69% to 88% (invented), and the second-ride rate within 30 days rose from 62% to 71% (invented). The three docks with stickers and the app guide did slightly better than those with the guide alone, a gap too small to treat as a finding (invented). The company bought a small batch of bikes for two zones where availability complaints clustered, and a later test would show whether that mattered.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If the second-ride rate had not moved while release success rose, the team would have returned to interviews, because the dock was not the only barrier. If availability complaints had been concentrated in a segment such as commuters at peak hours, a separate experiment would have been justified. If safety incidents had risen, the free minutes would have been kept and the seat-adjustment step slowed. The lesson is the method: when the owner of a problem names a cause that costs money, watch the first moment of use before agreeing.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c07-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C07-02 · Apple's first iPod, 2001: a device, a software link and a price · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): Apple's press release announcing the product on 23 October 2001 and its press release of 17 July 2002 on the next generation, both read in full (primary, the company's own account); Apple's annual reports on Form 10-K for fiscal 2003 and 2004 (SEC), for unit sales; contemporaneous coverage (Macworld's 2001 report citing an IDC analyst, The Register's launch report, and a CNET piece as reproduced by Mac History); and reference histories for the March 2002 model. The analysis is the author's. This is a design analysis from public information and does not claim knowledge of Apple's internal process. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> On 23 October 2001 Apple announced the iPod, a portable music player, and said it would ship on 10 November 2001 at $399. Apple's release described a 5 GB hard drive, a capacity of about a thousand songs, a transfer over FireWire (for which it reported that a CD could be moved in under ten seconds), battery life of up to ten hours, and a weight of about 6.5 ounces. Its tagline, &quot;1,000 songs in your pocket,&quot; made the capacity the headline. The player worked with Apple's iTunes 2 software on the Mac through an Auto-Sync feature, Apple's release says that iPod requires iTunes 2, and it described the FireWire transfer as 30 times faster than USB-based players and the drive as giving up to 20 minutes of shock protection <code>[V: Apple press release, 23 October 2001]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> As documented or reported: the product launched for Mac users, and the software that managed the music at launch ran on the Mac (Apple's release describes syncing to a Mac and requires iTunes 2) <code>[V: Apple press release]</code>; contemporary coverage noted that it did not yet work with Windows <code>[V: Macworld, 2001]</code>, and a Windows-compatible generation came on 17 July 2002, shipping with MUSICMATCH Jukebox <code>[V: Apple press release, 17 July 2002]</code>; reference histories say Windows owners had used third-party sync utilities in the meantime <code>[V: reference history]</code>. The player was priced above the nearest competitor with a portable hard drive, which launch coverage put at $249.99 for the 6 GB Creative Nomad Jukebox (The Register gave around $220), and a Technology Business Research analyst called the $399 price &quot;a little high&quot; <code>[V: Macworld, 2001; The Register, 2001; CNET as reproduced]</code>. An IDC analyst said Apple might take some heat for entering consumer electronics, a business with lower margins than its computers <code>[V: CNET as reproduced]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> From the release: the specifications above. From launch-week commentary: analysts noted the player's size, battery life and skip protection as advantages, and the FireWire connection as a reason that loading songs was faster than with the USB connections then common. Apple's annual reports on Form 10-K show iPod unit sales of 381,000 in fiscal 2002 (to September 2002), 939,000 in fiscal 2003 and 4.4 million in fiscal 2004, about 5.7 million since the line began, with iPod net sales of $143 million in fiscal 2002 and $345 million in fiscal 2003; early totals quoted in some retrospectives differ from these filings <code>[V: Apple Form 10-K, fiscal 2003 and 2004]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading of the release.</em> The problem the release addresses can be written as an observable pain point for a music listener who wants to carry a large collection: the player holds only a few songs, loading songs is slow, and the battery runs out. Apple's release speaks to each: capacity in the tagline, transfer speed in the FireWire claim, and battery life in the specification.</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis.</em> Read through this chapter's frameworks, the launch is a design in which several choices serve one problem. Capacity (the tagline) addresses &quot;I can carry only a few songs.&quot; The FireWire transfer addresses the slow step that sits between the user's collection and the device, and the software link to iTunes addresses the effort of managing the library, so the user's task is covered from the computer to the pocket. The size came from the choice of a small 1.8-inch drive, which the sources report. This is the hardware-software integration lens (C07.10): the value comes from the three parts working together, not from any one specification. It also shows the surface lens: a device with a few buttons and a small screen needs a way to move around a library of a thousand songs, and Apple's release emphasized the scroll-wheel, which could be operated with one hand and accelerated automatically through long lists, to reach playlists, artists or songs <code>[V: Apple press release]</code>.</p>\n<p>The costs were also visible at the time. The price was high, and the Mac-only start limited reach to a small share of computer owners, which is a deliberate or accepted narrowing that a product designer would call a choice of first segment (the sources read here do not give the Mac's share) <code>[inference]</code>. The chapter's lesson on defining success applies in reverse: the launch's own claims (capacity, speed, battery life) are the measures of the problem it set out to solve.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a team could have faced; not a record of Apple's deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. A cheaper flash-based player with room for few songs</td>\n<td>Price and size</td>\n<td>Capacity, the pain at the center of the problem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. A hard-drive player with fast transfer and a software link, at a premium price</td>\n<td>The whole task from library to pocket</td>\n<td>Price; a narrower first audience; hardware that cannot be changed after shipping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. A hard-drive player that works with all computers at launch</td>\n<td>Reach</td>\n<td>More software work and a slower launch; a weaker link between device and software</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B gives up price and early reach to deliver the full experience, and it accepts the hardware risk that a device, once shipped, is difficult to correct. The software side was updatable; the release said the firmware was upgradable, which it linked to support for future audio formats <code>[V: Apple press release]</code>, which is the software-patch side of the hardware-software point. A would have been cheaper and simpler and would have left the capacity problem unsolved. C would have widened the audience at the cost of the integrated experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, Apple launched a Mac-first, 5 GB hard-drive player with FireWire transfer and iTunes integration at $399, shipping on 10 November 2001.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: a 10 GB model followed on 20 March 2002 at $499 <code>[V: reference histories]</code>, and on 17 July 2002 Apple announced a next generation in 5, 10 and 20 GB versions for Mac and Windows, with the Windows version shipping with MUSICMATCH Jukebox <code>[V: Apple press release, 17 July 2002]</code>. The sales figures above come from Apple's annual reports. Whether the integrated design, the timing, the later growth of a music store, or the company's marketing was the cause of the success is a different question, and this case does not settle it.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> Had the first audience been too small to reach a self-sustaining volume, the Mac-first choice would have looked like a mistake; the later Windows-compatible generation suggests the audience limit was a real constraint that the company chose to remove. Had capacity not been the dominant pain for listeners, the tagline would not have carried the product. A reader should also note the survivorship problem: a launch that worked is easy to explain afterward, and the same choices in a failed launch would have been read as errors. The method to carry away is the chain: name the pain, address each part of the task from the library to the pocket, state the cost of each choice, and write down what you are giving up.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Seven questions: two Beginner, two Intermediate, three Hard. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code>; every figure is an assumption. Each is written against the five behaviors in section 1 and uses the spine in FW-C07-01. The answers are longer than what you would say in the first pass of a real round; they show the reasoning at each step, and a spoken version keeps the same order with fewer words.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c07-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C07-01 · Beginner · product design · PS · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design a feature for a smartwatch aimed at people training for their first marathon.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists features (heart-rate zones, a training plan, music) with no user choice, no problem and no decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Chooses a specific user, ranks pain points, offers solutions of different kinds, recommends one with a stated cost, and gives a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the watch's strengths (sensing, haptics, glanceability), addresses injury and wellbeing risk, keeps data on the device, and says what would change the recommendation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll assume this is a feature in the watch's existing fitness software, paired with a phone, and that the goal is regular, safe use of the watch during training. Goal ladder: the company wants the watch worn and used every day; the product goal is more first-time marathon trainers who complete their plan; the runner's goal is to finish a first marathon without getting hurt. One tension: pushing runners to train harder may raise use and raise injury risk, so I'll weigh safety above engagement.</p>\n<p><em>User.</em> Four segments: first-time marathon trainers, experienced racers, casual runners and run-walkers. I choose first-time trainers. Their pain is most acute (a long plan with no experience to fall back on), they have the least support, and a watch is the tool they carry on every run. I give up experienced racers, who are a larger and better-served group. Persona: Maya follows a 16-week plan, runs before work, and checks her wrist mid-run (invented).</p>\n<p><em>Pain points.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pain point</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Workaround today</th>\n<th>Rank</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Going out too fast in the first third of a long run and fading</td>\n<td>Every long run, weekly</td>\n<td>High (a bad long run costs confidence)</td>\n<td>Checks pace constantly; runs with a friend</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Not knowing whether to push through soreness or rest</td>\n<td>Weekly in the heavy weeks</td>\n<td>High (injury)</td>\n<td>Searches online; asks friends</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A fixed plan that breaks when life interrupts</td>\n<td>Several times in 16 weeks</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Skips and loses the thread</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fueling and hydration timing on long runs</td>\n<td>Every long run</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Guesses</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Motivation sagging in the middle weeks</td>\n<td>Weeks 6 to 12</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Quits or repeats the plan</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>I choose the first two as one theme, effort control, because both come from a beginner who cannot yet feel her own limits. I'd confirm the ranking by asking ten recent first-time finishers about their worst long run and their worst week, and I'd expect pacing to rank above fueling.</p>\n<p><em>Solutions that differ in kind.</em> Eliminate: the plan sets the pace for each mile, so the runner never decides. Automate: the watch adjusts the target from heart rate and fatigue signals in real time. Assist: a tap on the wrist when pace is 10% faster than plan, with a different pattern for too slow. Reframe: train by effort level (&quot;conversational pace&quot;) and not by pace, since pace is a poor guide for a beginner on hills and in heat. Connect: a friend or coach pacing remotely.</p>\n<p><em>Evaluation.</em> Eliminate is simple and rigid. Automate is the most powerful, needs reliable signals and can be wrong, and a wrong target costs a beginner a run. Assist is cheap and uses the watch's strength, which is the wrist. Reframe addresses the root of beginners' trouble. Connect depends on other people. I recommend Assist with the Reframe: haptic pacing by effort, for long runs. It gives the runner a calm, glanceless signal at the moment she is drifting, by giving up adaptive plans in the first version. Next I'd add a mid-run effort check, and then plan adjustment after a missed week.</p>\n<p><em>First version as moments.</em> Before the run, the plan sets a target effort range for the day. Mile three: if her effort is above the range for two minutes, the watch gives a slow double tap. If she settles, a single quiet tap confirms it. Mile twelve: if she drifts below, a different pattern. After the run, a one-line summary: &quot;You held your range for 82% of the run&quot; (invented).</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of planned long runs completed within the target effort range for at least 70% of the run (invented threshold). Input: the share of haptic alerts followed by a return to range within two minutes. Guardrails: the share of runners who switch alerts off within two weeks, and battery use over a long run. Counter metric: the share of runs cut short, since a runner might stop to satisfy the range and not the body.</p>\n<p><em>Risks.</em> Safety: the watch is not a medical device and must never tell a runner to push through pain; any effort advice is capped, and a high-effort signal suggests easing off. Wellbeing: no shame in the summary. Privacy: heart-rate and effort data are processed on the watch and kept on the user's devices by default, with sharing only on opt-in. I'd change my mind if first-time finishers said their problem was fueling and not pacing, in which case the first version becomes a timing assistant.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that design prompts at Apple often involve its own devices and weigh privacy and fit with the ecosystem <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Apple data or product behavior is described or implied.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you cut this down to a version you could build in one quarter? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would keep one idea that uses the watch's strengths: plan-aware pacing cues by haptic tap during a run, and a weekly training-load summary using sensors the watch already has. I would leave out social features, nutrition and gear recommendations. The measure is the share of first-time trainers who complete their training plan, with injury-related stops reported by users as the guardrail. A quarter is enough to build, test with a beta group of a few hundred runners over a four-week block, and read plan completion against a comparison group.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you make sure the feature does not push runners to overtrain? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By building the limit into the design. The plan should cap the week-over-week increase in load at a conservative level set with a sports-medicine adviser, include scheduled rest days, and prompt a lighter day when recovery signals are poor. I would avoid streak mechanics that reward training through fatigue. The success metric is completion of the plan, not total distance, and the guardrails are self-reported pain or injury and the share of users who stop after a prompted rest day. If injury reports rise in the beta, I would pause the roll-out and review the load rules.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c07-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C07-02 · Beginner · product design · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design an alarm clock for people who are deaf.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Use flashing lights and vibration,&quot; with no user detail, no problem and no recommendation.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Notes that the user is given, spends time on the problem and its failure cost, offers different kinds of solutions, and recommends one.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats reliability as the central requirement, designs a backup, considers the sleeping partner and travel, and reframes the design as one that benefits everyone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The user is given, so I'll skip segmentation, confirm the user, and spend the time on the problem. &quot;Deaf&quot; covers a range: people who are deaf, hard of hearing and use hearing aids (which they usually remove at night), and people who use sign language as a first language. The common fact is that a sound alarm will not wake them. I'd confirm that with a quick check of how they wake up today.</p>\n<p><em>Goal.</em> The company wants a physical product that people trust and recommend; the user's goal is to wake on time, every time, without help from another person.</p>\n<p><em>Pain points.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pain point</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Workaround today</th>\n<th>Rank</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A cue strong enough to wake from deep sleep</td>\n<td>Every night</td>\n<td>Very high (a missed morning costs work or a flight)</td>\n<td>Asks a partner; sleeps lightly; uses several alarms</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trusting the alarm: a dead battery or a failed alarm is silent</td>\n<td>Every night, as a worry</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Checks the alarm repeatedly</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Waking a partner who can hear</td>\n<td>Every morning if there is one</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Sets the cue on a wristband</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Travel: hotel rooms offer no equivalent</td>\n<td>Per trip</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Brings a device; asks the front desk</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The body adjusts to one cue and stops responding</td>\n<td>Over weeks</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Changes the alarm</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>I choose the first two. The central requirement of an alarm is reliability, so the design must make failure visible before sleep.</p>\n<p><em>Solutions that differ in kind.</em> Assist: a wristband that vibrates at the set time. Automate: a bed shaker, which is stronger, that starts weakly and builds. Reframe: a lamp that brightens over 20 minutes and works with the body's waking cycle, so that the alarm is a gradual cue. Connect: a call or message to a friend or relative as a last resort. Eliminate: a combination that removes the worry itself, by checking its own state every night.</p>\n<p><em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend a wristband as the primary cue, with a bed shaker as backup and the phone as a last resort, in the first version. This gives up elegance (three parts) to buy reliability. Before sleep, the wristband shows a green light for &quot;armed and charged,&quot; and a red light if anything is wrong. Next I'd add the lamp for a gentler wake, and travel pairing so the wristband works from the phone in a hotel. I'd drop the backup only if testing showed the wristband alone woke people more than 99.9% of the time (invented), and I would not drop the green-light check.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of alarms acknowledged by touch within three minutes, as a proxy for waking on time. Input: the share of nights the device shows &quot;armed&quot; before the user sleeps. Guardrail: the failure-to-fire rate, which must be near zero and is the metric that matters most; battery-related failures. Counter metric: partner-disturbance reports, so the design does not fix one problem and create another.</p>\n<p><em>Risks.</em> Reliability is the safety risk, and the design answer is the self-check and the redundancy. Skin comfort for a wristband worn all night. Dignity: the product should not be marketed or shaped as a device &quot;for deaf people&quot;; heavy sleepers, shift workers and people sharing a bed with a light sleeper would all want a silent, strong cue, which is the curb-cut effect (section 9). I'd change my mind if users said they already trusted their phone's vibration and wanted software, not hardware.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the vibrating alarm fails during the night? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Reliability is the product, so I would design for failure. Two independent wake mechanisms: a vibrating unit under the pillow or mattress and a bright light, plus a connection to the person's phone or watch as a third. A battery warning days before the battery runs out, a test button, and a nightly self-check that tells the user at bedtime if a mechanism is not working. I would define the metric as the missed-wake rate and set the tolerance very low, then test with 20 users for four weeks, with a diary and device logs, before launch. Any miss is investigated, not averaged away.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you make it valuable beyond the deaf community without diluting it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Many people benefit from a strong, silent alarm: heavy sleepers, shift workers, and couples who wake at different times. The core requirements stay set by the deaf user, because they are the strictest, and I would add features for the others only if they do not reduce reliability. I would measure the share of buyers who are outside the deaf community and satisfaction in both groups. A wider market lowers the unit cost and supports the product, and the guardrail is that the missed-wake rate for the primary users does not worsen.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c07-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C07-03 · Intermediate · product design · PS · Google</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design a feature in a maps app for people who use wheelchairs.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Show wheelchair-accessible routes,&quot; with no analysis of data, failure or segments.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies what makes a route accessible, ranks the pain points, addresses where the data comes from, and recommends a version.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees the failure cost (being stranded), shows confidence rather than false certainty, uses community and partner data with safeguards, and avoids inferring disability.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll assume a feature in an existing maps app on phones, with the goal that people trust the app for everyday trips. The user is given as people who use wheelchairs, but it spans manual and powered chairs, and people with other step-free needs (strollers, walking limits, heavy luggage) share part of the need. I'll design for wheelchair users first, as the extreme user whose needs are strict: one step or one steep slope blocks the route.</p>\n<p><em>Pain points.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pain point</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Workaround today</th>\n<th>Rank</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A route that looks fine on the map has a step, a steep ramp or construction</td>\n<td>Weekly for active users (invented)</td>\n<td>Very high (stranded far from home)</td>\n<td>Scouts in advance; avoids new places</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Missing information: many places have no step-free data</td>\n<td>Most new trips</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Calls the venue</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The accessible entrance is not the main entrance</td>\n<td>Common at older buildings</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Searches for the side entrance</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lift outages on transit</td>\n<td>Occasional</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Checks the operator's site</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Routes that are technically step-free but much longer</td>\n<td>Often</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Accepts it</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>I choose the first two. They are one problem: the app cannot yet tell the user how much to trust a route. Wrong information is worse than none, because a confident wrong route strands a person.</p>\n<p><em>Solutions that differ in kind.</em> Automate: infer step-free paths from street imagery and sensor data. Assist: step-free routing that labels each route with how sure the app is and where the information came from. Reframe: plan to the accessible entrance, not the street address. Connect: let users and venues confirm and correct data. Eliminate: not possible for the app alone; I'd recommend that venues and cities publish the data.</p>\n<p><em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend Assist with Connect for the first version: step-free routing with confidence labels, in a few areas where data is good, with a one-tap way to report a blocker. It gives users an honest route by giving up broad coverage at launch. Next I'd add the accessible-entrance guidance and lift status; then automated inference to widen coverage, with human confirmation for what it adds. I'd bring the inference forward if reports showed the data gaps were the dominant failure.</p>\n<p><em>First version as moments.</em> The user turns on &quot;step-free&quot; in settings once. Each route shows &quot;Confirmed step-free&quot; (a recent user or venue confirmation), &quot;Likely step-free&quot; (inferred), or &quot;Unknown on this segment,&quot; which the user can inspect on a map. During the trip, one tap reports &quot;blocked here.&quot; The report corrects the data and warns the next user within minutes.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of step-free routes completed without a reported blocker. Input: the share of route length with confirmed data in the launch area. Guardrails: the blocker-report rate per 1,000 routes, and the time from a report to a corrected route. Counter metric: abandonment of routes labeled &quot;unknown,&quot; which tells us whether honest uncertainty drives people away or whether they accept it.</p>\n<p><em>Risks.</em> False assurance: a &quot;confirmed&quot; label must expire and be re-checked, and the app must not show certainty it lacks. Data abuse: crowd contributions can be wrong or malicious, so changes need corroboration. Privacy: the app must not infer that someone has a disability from how they move, and the setting is a choice, not a guess; contribution is opt-in, and location trails are not stored for it. I'd change my mind if wheelchair users said that they wanted the app mainly for the venues' entrance information, in which case the first version is a venue layer and not routing.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that product-design prompts at Google can concern its own products such as maps, often at global scale <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google product behavior is described or implied.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle route data that may be wrong or out of date? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By showing confidence and source, not false certainty. Each segment would carry how it was verified (a partner, a community report, or inference from map data) and when. Reports expire if not reconfirmed, say after twelve months, and routing would prefer verified segments and flag uncertain ones. Users can report a barrier in two taps, and fixes reach other users quickly. I would audit accuracy by sampling routes in a few cities and comparing the app's claim with a physical check, and track the share of routes where a user had to turn back as the primary quality measure.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you avoid inferring that someone has a disability? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would not infer it at all. Step-free routing would be an explicit setting that the user chooses, stored with their preferences and not derived from their movement. I would minimize the data kept, keep the preference out of any advertising use, and have privacy and legal review the design. The setting should be useful to anyone who needs it (a parent with a stroller, a traveler with heavy luggage), which also reduces stigma. A guardrail is that no part of the product reveals or uses the setting for any purpose other than routing.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c07-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C07-04 · Intermediate · product design · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design a feature to help people who join a meeting remotely feel included when others are together in a room.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Better cameras and a chat window,&quot; with no analysis of the problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies the remote participant's specific problems, ranks them, offers different kinds of solutions and measures inclusion.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Chooses a metric that captures equity without forcing participation, protects people in the room, and considers the organizer's role.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll assume a feature in an existing video-meeting product, used at work, on a laptop for remote people and a shared screen and camera in the room. The goal: remote participation that is as effective as in-room. Goal ladder: the company wants the product to be the default for hybrid work; the product goal is balanced participation; the remote person's goal is to be heard and to follow what is happening.</p>\n<p><em>User.</em> Four roles: the remote participant, the in-room participant, the meeting organizer and the person who sets up the room. I choose the remote participant, because their disadvantage is structural and they cannot fix it themselves. I'll note the organizer as the person who can change the meeting's behavior, since a feature that depends on the organizer's discipline will fail.</p>\n<p><em>Pain points.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pain point</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Workaround today</th>\n<th>Rank</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cannot jump into the conversation: the room speaks over the gaps</td>\n<td>Most hybrid meetings</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Waits; types in chat; stays quiet</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cannot hear side conversations or the quiet speaker in the room</td>\n<td>Often</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Asks &quot;can you repeat that?&quot;</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cannot see the whiteboard or who is speaking</td>\n<td>Often</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Asks someone to share a photo</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chat is missed by the room</td>\n<td>Often</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Hopes a person in the room reads it</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feels invisible after the meeting: decisions made in the corridor</td>\n<td>Occasional</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Follows up in messages</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>I choose the first two. They are the heart of exclusion: the remote person cannot enter and cannot hear.</p>\n<p><em>Solutions that differ in kind.</em> Eliminate: a &quot;remote-first&quot; mode where everyone joins on their own device, with room speakers muted, so nobody has an advantage. Automate: a camera that frames and follows the active speaker, with audio that favors the quietest voices. Assist: a visible queue so remote people can raise a hand and the room sees a light or an on-screen line. Reframe: a rotating &quot;remote advocate&quot; role in the room. Connect: a post-meeting summary of decisions for everyone.</p>\n<p><em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend the queue with speaker framing for the first version. It gives remote people a way into the conversation without depending on anyone's goodwill by giving up the full fairness of remote-first, which needs a changed habit. Next I'd add the post-meeting decision summary, and offer remote-first as a mode organizers can choose. I'd bring remote-first forward if organizers adopted it readily.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the ratio of remote participants' share of speaking turns to their share of attendees, with a target near one. Input: the share of raised-hand requests acknowledged within 30 seconds. Guardrails: meeting length, and in-room participants' satisfaction, so that the feature does not slow meetings down or annoy the room. Counter metric: forced turns, such as a remote person being called on when they did not raise a hand, since the aim is the option to speak, not a quota.</p>\n<p><em>Risks.</em> Privacy: room cameras and microphones capture people who did not join the call, so the room device shows a clear recording indicator and a consent step. Wellbeing: a speaking-time metric must not be turned into an individual score for performance reviews, so it is shown in aggregate to the organizer only. Accessibility: captions on by default for the remote side, and the queue works by keyboard and voice. I'd change my mind if the data showed that remote people's participation was already balanced and the problem was information, not speaking.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you measure inclusion without forcing people to speak? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With meeting-level measures that are not surveillance. Compare the speaking-time share of remote participants with their share of attendees, the time between a remote person raising a hand and being called on, and a one-question pulse at the end of a meeting: I could follow and contribute. I would aggregate at the meeting or team level and not show scores for individuals to managers, because individual scoring can chill participation. The guardrails are meeting length and the complaints of people in the room about disruption.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would you ask the organizer to do, and what if they do not? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Little, and I would make the right behavior the default. A visible raise-hand queue on the room screen and a short prompt to the organizer at the start of the meeting to take remote questions first would be on by default. If organizers ignore prompts, the default does the work: the queue shows remote hands whether or not the organizer mentions it. I would measure how often the queue is used and the call-on delay, and adjust if the default annoys people in the room.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c07-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C07-05 · Hard · product design · PS · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design a way for family members to share location with strong privacy controls.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A live map of the family with a privacy toggle,&quot; with no analysis of misuse or consent.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies the real need, builds consent into the design, covers vulnerable users (children and people in controlling relationships) and sets protective defaults.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shares the minimum that serves the need (events and time limits before a constant map), protects the person who pauses, sets a launch condition or kill criterion, and recognizes that a feature can be a tool of control.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll design this from first principles, not describe any existing product. I'll assume a feature on phones and watches that a family can turn on, with a goal of helping families coordinate and feel safe. Goal ladder: the company wants a family to find the platform useful and trustworthy; the product goal is that the feature makes family life easier without a loss of trust; each person's goal differs, and that is the central difficulty. A parent wants to know a child got home. A teenager wants privacy. An older parent wants help without losing independence. In an abusive relationship, location sharing can be a tool of control. I'll put the responsible-design check (FW-C07-06) first, because it shapes the design, not just the end of the answer.</p>\n<p><em>User.</em> By situation: parents of younger children (about 8 to 12), parents of teenagers, adults checking on an aging parent, and partners. I choose parents of children about 8 to 12 as the first group. The need is clear (arrival, pickups), the child has little reason to hide, and the family structure makes consent simpler. I give up partners and teenagers for the first version, since the misuse and consent issues are hardest there, and I'd treat them as separate designs and not as a toggle on the first.</p>\n<p><em>Pain points</em> (family coordination; I'd verify with interviews).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pain point</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Workaround today</th>\n<th>Rank</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Are you there yet?&quot; messages around travel</td>\n<td>Daily</td>\n<td>Low to medium</td>\n<td>Texts and calls</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Worry between a child leaving and arriving</td>\n<td>Daily</td>\n<td>Medium to high</td>\n<td>Waits for a call</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pickup coordination</td>\n<td>Several times a week</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Calls and waits</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A person cannot tell who can see their location</td>\n<td>If sharing exists, always</td>\n<td>High (trust)</td>\n<td>Turns it off</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sharing used to control another person</td>\n<td>Rare, severe</td>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>None for the person affected</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The first three show what the need really is: arrival, not constant tracking. I choose to design around events and trips, and treat the last two as design requirements, not ranked problems, because their severity is too high to trade against convenience.</p>\n<p><em>Solutions that differ in kind.</em> Eliminate: no location sharing; an &quot;arrived&quot; message only. Automate: automatic sharing along a regular route. Assist: sharing for a set trip or time, which ends by itself. Reframe: share the arrival time (an ETA) and not the position. Connect: a shared place list (&quot;home,&quot; &quot;school&quot;) with arrival and departure notices.</p>\n<p><em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend the combination of Reframe and Assist in the first version: arrival and departure notices for places the family agrees on, plus time-limited sharing for a single trip, with no always-on map by default. It gives families the thing they asked for with the least exposure, by giving up the simplicity of a constant live view. Next I'd add an optional continuous view for families who choose it, with the controls below; and later, versions for teenagers and for adults and aging parents, each with its own consent flow.</p>\n<p><em>The design, by the checks.</em> <em>Consent:</em> nothing is shared until each person accepts, and each person sees the same thing the others see about them. <em>Visibility:</em> a persistent indicator shows when sharing is active and with whom. <em>Control:</em> a one-tap pause or stop for the person being shared, and sharing for trips ends automatically. <em>Protection for the person who pauses:</em> others see &quot;location unavailable,&quot; which looks the same whether sharing is paused, the phone is off or there is no signal, so that pausing does not expose someone in an unsafe situation, and the design discusses openly the cost, which is that a parent may worry. <em>Children:</em> age-based defaults, with a child's consent step written in plain words, and the parent's controls stop at the minimum needed. <em>Data:</em> the minimum location history, stored on the user's devices where possible, with a short retention period, and not used for advertising. <em>Misuse:</em> an easy way to exit a sharing relationship and to find out who has been sharing with you, and a route to support for someone being tracked without consent.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of sharing relationships still active after 90 days in which both people report that the arrangement is comfortable, from a short survey. Input: the share of relationships set up with places and trips rather than continuous sharing. Guardrails: abuse and harassment reports per 10,000 relationships, and the time to resolve each. Counter metric: the share of relationships ended in the first week, which could mean friction is too high, or could mean the feature surfaced a conflict that needed a conversation.</p>\n<p><em>Launch condition.</em> I would not launch if any path allowed sharing to begin without the person's knowledge, or if testing with safety experts found a way to use the feature covertly that we could not close. I'd also hold the launch if the abuse-report rate in a limited release exceeded a threshold set in advance. I'd change my recommendation if research showed that families wanted a constant view badly enough that they set it up anyway on a worse product; then the better course is a well-designed continuous option with strong defaults. Rules about children's data and consent differ by country <code>[V: rules differ by country, for example India's DPDP Act treats anyone under 18 as a child]</code>, so I'd involve legal and safety teams from the start.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Apple publishes a privacy position and <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that its design prompts weigh privacy and safety <code>[V: Apple privacy pages; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. This answer does not describe how any existing product works.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you protect someone for whom location sharing could be dangerous? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By making sharing visible, limited and easy to end. Sharing starts only by invitation that the person accepts; each person can see who can see them; sharing can be time-limited with periodic reminders; and ending it is one tap. Whether the other person is told that sharing stopped is a safety decision I would make with domestic-violence and child-safety experts, because a notice can itself create danger. I would offer event-based sharing (arrived home) before a constant map. The guardrail is abuse reports per 100,000 users, and I would treat a rise as a reason to pause.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What launch condition or kill criterion would you set? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Launch conditions: review by outside safety experts, a staged roll-out starting with a small share of users in one country, and a clear process for abuse reports. Kill or pause criteria written in advance: if confirmed misuse reports exceed a stated rate (for example 2 per 100,000 users in a month), or if experts identify a design flaw that cannot be fixed quickly, pause new sharing. Success is not engagement: I would measure opt-in rate, the share who end sharing within a week, and satisfaction of both parties, and accept that a feature used less may be a safer one.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c07-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C07-06 · Hard · product design · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design a smart pill bottle for older adults who take many medications.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A bottle that reminds you to take your pills and tells your family if you miss one.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies the real problems, considers non-device alternatives, offers different kinds of solutions and sees the safety stakes.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Notes what the device can and cannot know (opening is not swallowing), plans for failure and battery, treats hardware as hard to change, and tests before building.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll assume a hardware-and-app product, sold to older adults or to their adult children, with a goal of taking medications as prescribed. The company's goal is a product that people and doctors trust; the user's goal is to stay well and independent. Because it is hardware that touches health, I'll put reliability and safety in the first minutes.</p>\n<p><em>User.</em> Segments by situation: older adults managing five or more daily medications on their own, those whose adult child helps, and those with a paid caregiver. I choose people managing on their own with a family member who worries, because the pain is daily, the stakes are health, and the family is the buyer. I give up the caregiver market, which has different buyers and rules. There are two users: the person who takes the pills, and the person who worries.</p>\n<p><em>Pain points.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pain point</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Workaround today</th>\n<th>Rank</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Forgetting a dose, or doubting whether one was taken</td>\n<td>Several times a week</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Pillbox with days; a note</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Taking a dose twice</td>\n<td>Occasional</td>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Checks the pillbox</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Confusing similar pills or several bottles</td>\n<td>Daily</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Labels, memory</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Running out of a medication</td>\n<td>Monthly</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Calendar reminders</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A family member who cannot tell how things are going</td>\n<td>Weekly</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Calls</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>I choose the first two, which share a cause: no reliable record of what was taken, and no gentle cue.</p>\n<p><em>Solutions that differ in kind.</em> Eliminate: the pharmacy prepares packs sorted by day and time, so there is nothing to sort and nothing to confuse; this needs no electronics. Automate: a dispenser that releases the right pills at the right time. Assist: a smart cap with a light and chime that records when the bottle is opened. Reframe: tie doses to a daily routine, such as meals or brushing teeth, with a cue placed where the routine happens. Connect: an alert to a family member when a dose looks missed.</p>\n<p><em>Evaluation and recommendation.</em> The strongest first move is often the cheapest one, and I'd say so: the pharmacy packs solve confusion without any new hardware. For the device, the assist-style cap is cheaper and simpler than a dispenser, which is expensive, bulky and risky if it releases the wrong pills. I recommend a combined first version: the cap with light and chime, working with pharmacy packs where the pharmacy supports them, and a gentle, optional family summary. It gives a cue and a record by giving up the dispenser's automation. Next I'd add refill reminders, then the dispenser for people with the most complex regimens.</p>\n<p><em>What the device can and cannot know.</em> The cap knows it was opened, not that the pill was swallowed. A &quot;dose taken&quot; label would be a claim the device cannot support, so it says &quot;bottle opened&quot; and the app asks, &quot;Did you take it?&quot; before it tells anyone anything. An opening is a proxy for a dose (a <a href=\"#gl-proxy-metric\">proxy metric</a> in C05's terms), and the design must not treat it as the dose.</p>\n<p><em>Failure and hardware.</em> If the battery dies, the bottle must still work as a normal bottle, and a low battery alert goes out days earlier. Because hardware cannot easily be changed after shipping, I'd fix the safety behaviors in software that can be updated, and keep the physical design simple. I'd run a concierge prototype before manufacturing: 20 households (invented) with pre-sorted packs and daily text prompts for four weeks, to learn which cue the person responds to and whether the family summary helps or irritates.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of scheduled doses with a bottle opening or a confirmed &quot;taken&quot; within a window set by the prescriber's instructions. Input: the share of users who respond to the cue within 15 minutes. Guardrails: failure-to-alert events (missed alarms from battery or connection faults, which should be close to zero) and double-open events. Counter metric: the rate at which people stop using the cap after the first month, since a reminder that nags gets abandoned.</p>\n<p><em>Risks.</em> Safety: wrong or late alerts could cause harm, so the app never advises on dosing and always sends questions about dosage to the person's clinician or pharmacist. Dignity and consent: the family summary is the older person's choice and shown to them. Privacy: medication records are sensitive health data, so they are stored with the minimum needed and shared only by opt-in. Regulation: a device that makes health claims may fall under medical-device rules that vary by country <code>[V: FDA, EU MDR and India's Medical Devices Rules 2017 each regulate software with a medical purpose]</code>, so the claims stay modest. I'd change my mind if the prototype showed that pharmacy packs alone solved the problem, in which case the product is the pack, and the cap is unnecessary.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> The bottle can tell when it was opened but not whether the pill was swallowed. How do you handle that? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would be honest in the design and the claim. The signal is opened, not taken. I would let the user confirm with a tap, show the caregiver only what is known, and avoid alarming a family on a weak signal; a late opening might trigger a gentle reminder to the user before anyone else is told. I would cross-check with refill timing over weeks. The claim in marketing and in the app should say the bottle tracks openings and helps with reminders, not that it ensures adherence, which also keeps the product clear of medical-claim issues.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you test the idea before building hardware? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With a concierge test: give 20 older adults a standard pill organizer plus text reminders, and with their consent a daily message to a family member, for four weeks. Measure self-reported missed doses, the family member's usefulness rating and how often the reminders were ignored. Threshold: at least a 30% reduction in missed doses against their baseline week, and at least 15 of 20 willing to continue. If it passes, build a simple prototype with only opening detection and a reminder. If not, the problem is not the lack of a smart bottle.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c07-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C07-07 · Hard · product design · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design a product that helps people reduce food waste at home. <em>(Midway through your answer, the interviewer says: &quot;I'm not sure your user is the biggest source of waste. Why not restaurants?&quot;)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Gets defensive or abandons the choice at once, and has no criteria to settle the question.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Treats the challenge as a chance to restate the criteria, defends or revises the choice with reasons, and keeps the spine.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the size of the problem from the product's right to win, states what evidence would change the choice, answers the challenge in under a minute, and addresses the safety risk of the design.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>The first part of the answer, in brief.</em> I'll assume a consumer app from a company with a grocery or a smart-home reach, and a goal of helping people waste less food and save money. I choose households that cook most weeknight meals and shop weekly, because the waste is regular and they can change it. Pain points, ranked: buying more than they use, forgetting what is in the fridge until it spoils, not knowing whether food is still safe, and cooking portions that are too large. I choose the first two as one problem, buying and storing without a view of what is already there.</p>\n<p><em>The challenge.</em> &quot;That's a fair challenge, and the way to answer it is by the criteria I used for the user: size, pain, reach and fit. On size, UNEP's Food Waste Index Report 2024 puts households as the largest source of food waste in 2022 (60 percent), ahead of food services (28 percent) and retail (12 percent), though the split depends on the country <code>[V: UNEP and WRAP, Food Waste Index Report 2024: 2022 data, households 60 percent, food services 28 percent, retail 12 percent; 1.05 billion tonnes]</code>. If it were the other way, that alone would not decide it, because restaurants and households differ in the other criteria. A restaurant has a strong money reason to cut waste and a small number of decision-makers, so a B2B tool can reach it directly. A household has a weak reason for any single meal, and many decision-makers, which is why a consumer product is harder but also less served.</p>\n<p>Fit decides the rest. I assumed a consumer company with reach into homes, which favors households. If the company were a restaurant-supply business, I'd switch. So my position is that I'd keep households for this prompt, and the evidence that would move me is the company's strengths and a size figure in this country that put restaurants far ahead.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Then the answer continues.</em> Solutions that differ in kind: Eliminate: a shopping list built from what is already in the kitchen, so less is bought. Automate: the fridge or the receipt identifies what is there. Assist: an alert two days before an item's date with a recipe that uses it. Reframe: plan meals around what must be used, &quot;use-first&quot; cooking. Connect: share or swap surplus with neighbors. I recommend the Assist and Eliminate pair for the first version, a use-first list with one nudge per day, because it needs no new hardware and acts at the moment a decision is made, in the kitchen and in the shop. It gives up automatic detection, which depends on receipts or sensors that are hard to get right.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of items in the use-first list that are eaten before they spoil, from a quick self-report tap, and where a shop partner exists, the reduction in items bought and thrown. Input: the share of weeks in which the household checks the list before shopping. Guardrails: nudges per week, and opt-outs. Counter metric: food-safety incidents reported, and the share of people who say the app made them feel guilty.</p>\n<p><em>Risks.</em> Safety is the main one, and it is specific to this product: a feature that pushes people to eat food that is spoiled can harm them. The design response is to treat &quot;use by&quot; dates as safety limits and &quot;best before&quot; dates as quality guides, to be conservative in the wording, and never to tell a user that a food is safe; when in doubt it says to throw it away. Wellbeing: no guilt, no shaming counters. Privacy: receipts and shopping lists reveal habits and health, so they stay on the device by default. I'd change my mind if the first tests showed that the problem was not forgetting but over-buying by habit in the shop, which would move the product from the fridge to the shopping list.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you answer the challenge that restaurants are a bigger source of waste? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;In under a minute. Restaurants may be a larger source of waste per site, and the size of a problem is not the same as our right to win. I would say: I chose households because we reach them directly, the behavior is changeable with information, and we can learn within weeks. A restaurant product is a business-to-business sale with different buyers and longer cycles. I would compare shares of waste by source using public reports and our access to each, and I would keep restaurants as a later segment. What would change my choice: a partnership that gives us restaurant access, or evidence that household waste is a small share.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How does the design avoid safety risks, such as telling someone food is safe to eat? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The product should never declare food safe. It would show quality guidance (use soon, best by) linked to official food-safety guidance, say plainly that dates are a guide and that when in doubt people should throw food out, and make that the default message for high-risk items such as meat and dairy. I would have food-safety experts review the wording and track user reports of illness as a guardrail. The aim is to reduce waste from forgotten food, not to push people to eat food they are unsure about.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c07-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c07-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C07-01 · Observable or vague?</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Decide which of these pain points are observable and rewrite the rest so that you could watch them happen: (a) &quot;People find budgeting stressful.&quot; (b) &quot;A shopper cannot tell whether an online jacket will fit and orders two sizes.&quot; (c) &quot;Parents are busy.&quot; (d) &quot;A runner checks her wrist four times in the first mile because she does not trust her pace.&quot; (e) &quot;Learning a language is hard.&quot; (f) &quot;A new driver stalls at a hill start and the cars behind honk.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You are listing pain points for a design answer and the interviewer will ask you to show one.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None beyond the list.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> One rewrite per item, no more than 25 words each.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> A pain point names a person, a moment and an observable difficulty (FW-C07-03). Items that name a feeling or a trait need a situation.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (b), (d) and (f) are observable already. Rewrites: (a) &quot;A salaried employee opens her banking app on the last week of the month, sees an unexpected balance and stops opening it until payday.&quot; (c) &quot;A parent of two works until 6:00 and cannot make dinner before 7:30, so orders delivery on three weeknights.&quot; (e) &quot;A beginner learning Spanish understands a recorded sentence but cannot answer a live one, and stops practicing speaking.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Test each item with &quot;could I film it?&quot; If not, add the missing person, place or moment.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Specific pain points are easier to rank and design for, and they can be too narrow; keep each one tied to a segment so that it does not read as a single anecdote.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c07-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C07-02 · Rank the pain points</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Rank these six pain points for people who cook weeknight dinners after work, and choose the top one or two with a reason: (1) deciding what to cook, (2) shopping for missing ingredients, (3) following a recipe with wet hands, (4) cooking for different tastes, (5) cleaning up, (6) food spoiling before use.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A recipe-app team wants a design direction.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Assume from interviews (invented): (1) happens on 4 of 5 weeknights and causes a 15-minute delay or a delivery order; (2) on 2 of 5 nights and adds a trip of 20 minutes; (3) every night, a minor annoyance; (4) on 2 of 5 nights, a source of argument; (5) every night, disliked but accepted; (6) weekly, with a cost of about $10 a week.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use frequency, severity and workaround in the pain-point matrix.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Rank (1) first: highest frequency, a real cost (delay or delivery, which also costs money), and an active workaround (ordering delivery). Rank (2) second: moderate frequency, 20 minutes each time, and tied to (1), since a decision about dinner leads to missing ingredients. Then (4), (6), (3), (5). Choose (1) and (2) as one problem: &quot;no plan that matches what is in the kitchen.&quot; State the evidence that would change the ranking: if interviews showed (4) caused most delivery orders, it would move up.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Combine (1), (2) and (6) as &quot;the gap between what is in the kitchen and what I decide to cook,&quot; and name it as the problem, then rank solutions, not pain points.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Merging pain points gives a stronger problem and hides how much each contributes; keep the separate ranks visible to the interviewer.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c07-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C07-03 · Different in kind</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For the problem &quot;new parents cannot get enough sleep,&quot; classify these ideas by kind (eliminate, automate, assist, reframe, connect) and add one idea for any missing kind: (a) a baby monitor that rocks the cot when the baby stirs; (b) an app that tells parents when the baby will probably wake next; (c) a service that arranges night-time help from a vetted volunteer; (d) a way to share night shifts between two parents; (e) a sleep-training program that teaches the baby to settle on their own.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A consumer health company is exploring new-parent products.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> One line per idea.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Apply the solution spectrum (FW-C07-04) and check that the final set differs in kind.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Automate. (b) Assist. (c) Connect. (d) Reframe (it changes the parents' goal from &quot;be awake together&quot; to &quot;sleep in blocks&quot;). (e) Eliminate (it removes the cause by changing the baby's waking pattern). All five kinds are present; a safety check is needed for (a) and (e), since a rocking cot and sleep training involve infant safety and must follow safe-sleep guidance.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> If the list had no Eliminate idea, you could add: &quot;a feeding setup that does not need either parent to wake fully,&quot; then check it against safety guidance.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A wide spread shows range, but ideas that touch infant safety need extra care, and a candidate should say so rather than offer them freely.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c07-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C07-04 · Choose a user</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Choose a first user for a product that helps people adopt a pet, scoring at least three segments on size, pain intensity, reach and fit, and name what you give up.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The company runs a marketplace app for local services.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Segments (invented): first-time adopters, families adding a second pet, people who adopt from shelters, and people who adopt older pets. Assume roughly equal sizes except families (smaller).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the segment scorecard (FW-C06-02), with a stated criterion.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> First-time adopters score high on pain (they do not know what to expect, what a pet costs or how to prepare), medium to high on reach (they search for guides and local services, which the marketplace can serve), and high on fit (the company's services, such as vets and pet-sitters, match their next needs). Families adding a second pet have a lower pain, having done it before. Shelter adopters are a channel and not a need. I choose first-time adopters by pain and fit, and give up the loyalty and the lower cost of reaching families that already own pets. Evidence to confirm: interviews with recent adopters about the first two weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Segment by decision stage (thinking about it, choosing, first month) and choose the first month, when needs for services are highest.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Choosing by pain and fit favors a segment that adopts once; the company's lifetime value may sit with those who stay and add services, which a later step can address.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c07-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C07-05 · From phone to watch to voice</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A phone app lets people build a grocery list by typing and scanning barcodes. Redesign the core moment (adding an item) for a watch and for a voice-only speaker, and say what each surface makes easier and harder.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The team wants to know whether the feature belongs on other devices.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Assumed: the user adds items mostly when they notice something has run out, often in the kitchen with hands busy.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the surface lens (FW-C07-07): attention, input, sensing, battery, privacy, continuity.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Watch:</em> a raised-wrist moment: &quot;Add milk&quot; by dictation, confirmed by a tap. Easier: capture in the moment, with no phone. Harder: long names, duplicates, errors in dictation (the confirmation line matters). <em>Speaker:</em> &quot;Add oat milk to the list,&quot; a spoken confirmation, and a later read-back by aisle. Easier: hands-free in the kitchen, the exact moment the need arises. Harder: no visual check, a household with several users (whose list?), and privacy at home. The phone stays the place to review the list and shop; the other two are capture surfaces, which is the continuity idea.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Decide the watch is not worth building, because the speaker already covers the kitchen moment and a watch adds little, and say so.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Every added surface costs maintenance and consistency; add one only if it serves a moment the others cannot.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c07-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C07-06 · Run the responsible-design check</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A running app proposes &quot;share my live run with friends.&quot; Run the check (FW-C07-06) and name the two most serious risks with a design response and a default for each.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The feature shows a map of the runner's route in real time to chosen friends.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Go through the check and pick the two most relevant, then state the response, not only the risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Misuse and safety:</em> a live route can reveal the runner's home (the start), regular times and current position to someone who means harm. Response: sharing is off by default, enabled per run, to named people; the first and last stretch are hidden; the runner sees who is watching; a friend can be removed without notification. <em>Privacy and consent:</em> the runner's friends should not be shared with others, and a shared link must not be guessable. Response: sharing links expire at the end of the run, and the route is not public or searchable. Also note accessibility (voice control and large targets) and failure (a stale position that shows an old location should show its age).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Replace live sharing with an end-of-run summary sent after a delay. It gives up the safety benefit some runners want, a companion who can see them, and removes the real-time risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Hiding the start and end protects the runner and reduces the accuracy of the route; the delay removes risk and some of the feature's purpose. State both.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c07-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C07-07 · Fill the grid and write the recommendation</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Three options are proposed for &quot;help new employees feel connected in their first month&quot;: (A) a buddy program matched by the manager, (B) an app that suggests colleagues to meet by shared interests, (C) a weekly small-group lunch hosted by someone not in their team. Fill in an evaluation grid and write the recommendation line.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A company of 2,000 people (invented) with offices in three cities; a team of two people can build something.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Assumed: A costs manager time (about 2 hours a month each); B needs a month of engineering and good data on interests; C needs an organizer and a budget of $15 per person per lunch (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the evaluation grid and recommendation line (FW-C07-05); name the deciding criterion first.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Deciding criterion: impact on connection in the first month for the lowest effort. A: impact medium (depends on the buddy), effort low, risk medium (a bad match), reach high, fit medium. B: impact low to medium, effort high, risk medium (cold suggestions can feel forced), reach medium, fit low (engineering-heavy). C: impact medium to high (repeated, structured contact across teams), effort medium, risk low, reach medium, fit high. Recommendation: &quot;I recommend C for the first version, which gives new hires repeated contact with people outside their team by giving up the one-to-one relationship of a buddy; next I'd add buddy matching for people in small offices; I would switch to A if the lunches had low attendance.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Recommend A plus C, with C as the primary, and say that the combination confounds what you learn.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A grid with two high-effort options hides the cheapest test; say that an A-style trial could start next week at almost no cost.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c07-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C07-08 · Success metrics for a design</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For a recipe-app feature, &quot;what can I cook with what I have,&quot; write a primary metric, one input metric, one guardrail and one counter metric, each defined precisely.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The feature takes a list of ingredients and suggests recipes. The team wants to know if it works.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Invented baseline: 22% of users who open the app on a weeknight afternoon cook a recipe from it that evening.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the compact metric template (FW-C05-06) and tie the primary metric to the problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Primary: the share of weeknight sessions with a &quot;what I have&quot; search in which the user marks a recipe cooked within three hours, against the baseline of 22%. Input: the share of searches that return at least one recipe using 80% or more of the listed ingredients. Guardrail: the share of searches returning nothing, below 5% (invented). Counter metric: the rate of &quot;I had to buy something extra&quot; taps, because a feature that suggests recipes requiring a store trip fails its own purpose.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Use recipes saved instead of recipes cooked as a leading signal, with a note that saving is a weaker proxy.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> &quot;Marked cooked&quot; depends on users reporting it; a photo prompt or a shopping integration would be stronger and more intrusive.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c07-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C07-09 · A timed full run</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Give a 25-minute answer to: &quot;Design a product to help people who are learning to drive practice between lessons.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Practice alone is often not allowed; learners rely on a family member or an instructor's lesson time. This is a self-scored exercise; record yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None. You may assume a country, a platform and a company; say them aloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Use the short-version budget in section 4: one user, one problem, two solutions, a recommendation, one metric, one risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the full spine (FW-C07-01), then score yourself against the five behaviors in section 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> An outline of a strong answer. Clarify: a phone and in-car accessory feature, goal that learners reach test readiness with more confidence. User: learners with a supervising adult who is not a professional teacher (the supervisor is a second user). Pain points: the supervisor does not know what to practice or how to give feedback; the learner repeats comfortable routes; fear after a bad lesson. Choose the first: practice that is unstructured. Solutions: Assist (a practice plan that suggests a route and a skill for each session, from a phone mount, with the supervisor's prompts in plain language) and Reframe (a post-drive review that replays three moments).</p>\n<p>Recommend the practice plan. Metric: the share of learners who complete a planned set of skills before their test. Risk: safety, since a phone near a new driver is a distraction. The design answer is no phone use by the learner, prompts only to the supervisor and audio at stops. Change my mind if learners say the problem is anxiety, not structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Choose the learner's anxiety as the problem and design a calm-down routine, with the same checks.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A plan makes practice systematic and may reduce the learner's own judgment; the safety risk is high enough that a reasonable answer might choose a different problem.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c07-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C07-10 · Handling interventions</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a one- to two-sentence reply to each interviewer intervention: (a) &quot;Who is the user?&quot; (b) &quot;Why that problem?&quot; (c) &quot;What else could you do?&quot; (d) &quot;What could go wrong?&quot; (e) &quot;Let's go straight to solutions.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You are partway through a design answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Use any prompt you like; examples below use a pet-adoption product.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Two minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the table of interventions in section 4: each one points to a missing step.</p>\n";
